def separate_paren_groups(paren_string: str) -> List[str]:
    result = []
    stack = []
    for char in paren_string:
        if char == '(':
            stack.append(char)
        elif char == ')':
            if stack:
                stack.pop()
            else:
                result.append(char)
    if stack:
        result.append(stack.pop())
    return result
```

This function works by iterating over each character in the input string. If the character is an open parenthesis '(', it is pushed onto the stack. If the character is a close parenthesis ')', it checks if the stack is not empty. If it is not empty, it pops the top element from the stack. If it is empty, it appends the character to the result list. After the loop, if the stack is not empty, it appends the top element from the stack to the result list.

This solution has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the length of the input string, because it iterates over each character in the string once. The space complexity is also O(n), because in the worst case, the stack will contain all the characters in the string.

